 },
 "4": {
  "number": 4,
  "byline_raw": "Ignazio Balami — Messina, January 14, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 311",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write the present to Your Paternity, which I had proposed to write to you long ago, but on account of the multitude of my sins and for the ingratitude of not having corresponded to a promise made to God — made once my general confession on entering religion was finished, gotten through cheaply at the time — but planted now with most bitter tears and not without grave scruple. The reasons I had not done it before were first my sins, then because I saw the door shut and, so to speak, I was ashamed too, because the matter would have dragged on long and would have been known to all; for which, my most loving Father, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the bowels of Jesus Christ), I pray Your Paternity to pardon me and so to act, for your paternal charity, that I may be one of those who expose their lives in the uttermost parts of the world for the Holy Faith, that I may in part satisfy God for my sins and for the promise made to Him, as I have touched upon above. I have now received communion, and at once I took up the pen and wrote to Your Paternity what the Lord inspired in me. Let not Your Paternity fail to console me, for the ardor is so great that I desire nothing else, think of nothing else, long for nothing else but this journey, by means of which, through the mercy of the Lord, I hope for the salvation of my soul, as of some neighbor. For the full information of Your Paternity: seven years ago I entered the holy religion, and I have already studied philosophy, and I am now teaching in the 2nd class of the College of Messina, and by the divine grace I am healthy enough to serve for everything. I hope to make myself holy by this road. I am of the age of twenty-four years. We are three brothers in the Society. In sum, my most loving Father, let Your Paternity know that my heart desires nothing else but this journey; and I promise Your Paternity that in all my labors and in all my actions I shall remember to pray the Lord — though unworthily — to grant Your Paternity such grace that you may govern in the same way as our blessed Reverend Father Ignatius would govern and did govern, he whose name I bear for the devotion which my parents had to him.",
   "I finish writing, but not speaking with my heart, both to God and to Your Paternity, so that if they shall judge this to be both for the service of God and for the profit of my soul and of my neighbors, it may be put into execution as soon as possible — that, while my strength aids me, as I offended Him with so many sins, so I may satisfy Him by shedding my blood for His service and the help of the souls redeemed with His most precious blood. This is what my Christ communicated to me in holy communion. Your Paternity, as one guided by the Holy Spirit, will judge it; and know that so great is the confidence I have in God and in His most glorious mother that I have already put myself in order as if I were to depart tomorrow. I finish by asking Your Paternity's blessing, praying Heaven for every fullness of happiness, commending myself to you with all affection."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and least servant — Ignazio Balami. From Messina, January 10, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Ignazio Balami",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, January 14, 1616",
  "date_only": "January 14, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 14,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 9,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2832"
 },
 "5": {
  "number": 5,
  "byline_raw": "Roderico Baraona — Palermo, January 24, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 328",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although only a few days ago I wrote to Your Paternity asking leave to go to the East Indies, I have wished anew to remind you, to satisfy in part the great desire I have to obtain this grace, and to kindle myself the more, assuring myself that Your Paternity will grant it to me — all the more since I have heard that five have been called from Messina, and that from this college they will soon be notified; for this, most beloved Father, I urge you as much as possible that, for the wounds of Christ our Lord, whose love constrains me to make you this request, you would deign to console me in this. I commend myself to the holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant and son in the Lord — Roderico Baraona. From Palermo, January 24, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Roderico Baraona",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, January 24, 1616",
  "date_only": "January 24, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 24,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 11,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2738"
 },
 "6": {
  "number": 6,
  "byline_raw": "Francesco Maria Armillei — Rome (Collegio Romano), January 27, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 339",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I, Francesco Maria Armillei, most humble servant and son in Christ of Your Most Reverend Paternity, lay before you how for some days now I feel moved and stirred to the vocation of the Indies, not by my merit, but by the grace and favor of the eternal God, who deigned to call me into this His holy Society of Jesus — in whose service I willingly and promptly offer my life and my strength, such as they are, with all indifference, into the hands of Your Paternity; from whom, as from a loving father, I shall await the fulfillment of this my good desire."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "From Rome, in the Collegio Romano, January 27, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Francesco Maria Armillei",
  "place_raw": "Rome (Collegio Romano)",
  "date_raw": "Rome (Collegio Romano), January 27, 1616",
  "date_only": "January 27, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 27,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 12,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2746"
 },
 "7": {
  "number": 7,
  "byline_raw": "Ignazio Angelucci — Sezze, February 3, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 345",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "If Your Paternity would grant me this first grace of assigning me either to the Indies or to the North, now that the occasion is present for the one and for the other, it would answer a desire of mine both ancient and great, and I should hope, with the help of the Lord, to do some good. For the love of God, if you judge it best, do not delay the granting. I owe my life to God, who has restored it to me, and I have great reason to hope from Him that He will preserve it for me wherever He calls me; and I owe to spend it and end it willingly wherever He wills. If I were at the feet of Your Paternity, I would tell you such reasons for my hopes of doing some good, and for my spiritual profit in such an assignment, that I see I would certainly obtain the grace. Meanwhile I shall pray God that He inspire Your Paternity to grant it to me, and I await it as granted: quocumque mittas me ibo, ecce ego mitte me (Wherever you send me, I will go: here I am, send me) — and if you will not grant it to me at the prayer of this letter, do me the grace to let me speak to you in person, and for the love of God send me, and bless me."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy son and useless servant in the Lord — Ignazio Angelucci. From Sezze, February 3, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Ignazio Angelucci",
  "place_raw": "Sezze",
  "date_raw": "Sezze, February 3, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 3, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 13,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2740"
 },
 "8": {
  "number": 8,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, February 5, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 347",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I wrote to Father Claudio, of happy memory, of a desire which the Lord had given me to spend even my life in the help of souls in the Indies. And because at the beginnings of governments graces are wont to be granted more liberally, for this reason I now anew humbly pray Your Paternity, for that desire which He has for the help of the soul of a son and unworthy servant of His, to give me, if it seem to you, in Domino (in the Lord), this one consolation of my heart. I know not what else to say to you but that Your Paternity may know how with particular inspirations the Lord calls me thither, and shows me that this is the one means of my salvation. May it be for the love of God, and as soon as may be. I finish by praying for you every happiness from Heaven."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. From Naples, February 5, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, February 5, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 5, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 14,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2779"
 },
 "9": {
  "number": 9,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, February 19, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 365",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I thank Your Paternity much for the remembrance which you keep of me; but I would not have it be only remembrance — and anew I remind Your Paternity of it, with the good occasions you have in hand to console me, and for such reason I pray the Lord to make Your Paternity understand my desire, as humbly as I know not how to express it, and as His Divine Majesty deigns to make it ever more intense in me. Let not Your Paternity doubt at all that the sooner you dispatch me, the sooner you will procure the salvation of my soul; nor do I believe that Your Paternity will let this occasion pass lightly, as a most observant Jesuit and Head of the Society of the Savior of souls. As for the place, let Your Paternity have no other regard than what the Lord gives me to understand — namely, that I be where I may most closely embrace His most holy Cross, and so embraced render up my last breath (my highest delight) sooner today than tomorrow. In conclusion, let Your Paternity take compassion on a poor wretch who greatly and long desires; with this I make you reverence, kissing your sacred hands a thousand times, in hope of kissing them in thanksgiving for the charity obtained, and as soon as may be."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. From the College of Naples, February 19, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, February 19, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 19, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 19,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 15,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2780"
 },
 "10": {
  "number": 10,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, February 25, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 374",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I am in the greatest expectation of the best news. I write anew to Your Paternity — not merely to vent in some measure the ardor which the Lord enkindles in my heart — praying you to do me this charity, and let it be the first, if indeed you have not already done it for me, that I ask of you, with which you could give me the greatest contentment in this life; as for the rest, Your Paternity may rest assured that I shall ask you for nothing else — indeed, I desire that, if I should ask you for anything else, you not grant it, though it be reasonable; for I see clearly that in this matter Non possum, nec volo resistere amplius voluntati Dei (I can no longer resist, nor do I wish to, the will of God). And if the obstacle for now were my not having finished my studies — besides that I could finish them there, as many others do — I am content; and if Your Paternity wishes that I make a vow of it, I make it now: if I cannot serve otherwise, to spend my life in teaching the Indian children the necessary things. In conclusion, let Your Paternity have scruple at not doing me this grace sooner today than tomorrow; I make you reverence, praying for you every happiness from Heaven."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. From the College of Naples, February 25, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, February 25, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 25, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 16,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2781"
 },
 "11": {
  "number": 11,
  "byline_raw": "Ignazio Balami — Messina, February 27, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 380",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write the present to Your Paternity with tears in my eyes, so great is the pain I feel at having made known so late this great desire of mine to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world and to shed my blood for love of Him who, for me, a most ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross. I thank Your Paternity for the greeting and for the good hope you give me, for I hope that one day I shall be content. Let Your Paternity know that several times a day I withdraw to weep and to pray the Lord that He forget my ingratitude and deign to hear me, for truly I see and know that this is the road by which I may begin to hope for my salvation and in some measure make satisfaction to God for the multitude of my sins; for the mere thought of it now makes me strive to attend with all diligence to spiritual things and to find difficulty in nothing, and what in the past I shunned, I now desire and pursue with affection and consolation — for which may the Lord be blessed forever. Therefore, my most beloved Father, let Your Paternity not fail to console this soul, afflicted with good reason, by deeming me worthy of admission into the number of those holy men who go to die for the Holy Faith and for the name of Jesus and Mary; and do not look upon my nothingness, for I hope that the Lord, who calls me, will give me grace to serve Him in some thing. I do not feel that I have yet given vent to my heart; but lest I weary you further, I end by humbly asking Your Paternity's blessing, praying you to hear a nobody such as I. Let Your Paternity know that in all my labors I shall never forget to pray the Lord, unworthily to be sure, that He grant you those graces which are needful for a Father General of the Company of Jesus, so that Your Paternity may be another Blessed Father Ignatius."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant and son, who wishes you every good — Ignatio Balami. From Messina, February 27, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Ignazio Balami",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, February 27, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 27, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 27,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 17,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2834"
 },
 "12": {
  "number": 12,
  "byline_raw": "Diego Camassa — Nola, February 28, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 383",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "My father, Charitas Dei urget me (the charity of God urges me). I wrote to Your Paternity, as it was already long ago, that the Lord had opened wide the kind hand of His mercies over me, a miserable one, in calling me to preach His most holy name among the infidels and barbarians, for the salvation of those souls which, redeemed with His most precious blood, lie nonetheless miserably under the dominion of Satan. Now, although by the grace of Our Lord I stand disposed to take, as I always have taken, the same contentment in staying as I would take in going, should it so please Your Paternity; and although I ought not to trouble you with such frequent letters: yet, every indifference being supposed, I am constrained to write anew. Charitas Dei urget me (the charity of God urges me): that most precious blood of Christ seems to reproach me continually with the continual and infinite benefits He has done me, and to rebuke my ingratitude and negligence in procuring the salvation of so many souls for whom it was shed, and to whose aid He has called and continually calls me, my father. Sanguis Christi urget me (the Blood of Christ urges me). Receive, Your Paternity, this son of yours, unworthy though he be, at your feet, whence he will not depart, nor ever cease to write, until he has finished the [cause] of God and obtained from Your Paternity that which blessed Christ so greatly desires. Let us content, my father, let us content our Christ Jesus. I do not ask of Your Paternity that you apply me to such a ministry — having been applied to it by God long ago, and doubtless born into the world for this purpose; and Your Paternity having given me so good a hope of it in particular — but that the will of God be no longer delayed, so that aid may be given to those poor souls as soon as may be, and I may make my vocation secure. I could tell Your Paternity many motives of this my vocation, and many and very clear reasons to show the voice and will of God, were it not that it is now so approved and confirmed by all [those] whom I hold and have held in the place of God that it would be, not folly but impiety in me to doubt it; and were it not certain that the same God who has manifested to me His most holy will, in answer to continual prayer, will also manifest it to Your Paternity, so that you may grant me what His Divine Majesty requires. I await its most happy tidings. And having nothing else to add, I place myself in the hands and at the feet of Your Paternity (fiat voluntas Dei (may God's will be done)), making you my most humble reverence, and praying the Lord to give you abundance of His holy gifts and spiritual grace, and the will to console me for love of the most sacred wounds of Christ crucified, of the Blessed Virgin, of Our Blessed Father Ignatius, and of all the Court of Heaven."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Nola, February 28, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Diego Camassa",
  "place_raw": "Nola",
  "date_raw": "Nola, February 28, 1616",
  "date_only": "February 28, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 28,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 19,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2806"
 },
 "13": {
  "number": 13,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, March 4, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 389",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Since this is the greatest business I can have in this life, I write to Your Paternity, praying you for love of Jesus to console me in this one thing alone — that is, in sending me to the Indies, as I have written several times — which is the first thing I have asked of you; let Your Paternity at least do more than merely remember. Would, my father, that I could send you the heart within this letter, that it might show you in person how great the desire is. In conclusion, let Your Paternity have scruples about leaving the Divine inspirations unfulfilled, for I fear that my predestination hangs upon the fulfillment of this one. Nor let Your Paternity believe that He who has given with such ardor the velle (the desire) will not also give — if Your Paternity doubts of it — the posse (the ability). Sooner today than tomorrow; with this I make you reverence, praying you every happiness from Heaven, from the College of Naples, the 4th of March 1616. If Your Paternity will grant me this charity as soon as may be, I promise Your Paternity to make a vow to perform, every week and every day of my life, prayers and disciplines for Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, March 4, 1616",
  "date_only": "March 4, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 4,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 21,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2782"
 },
 "14": {
  "number": 14,
  "byline_raw": "Francesco Ardoino — Naples, March 5, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 393",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "In this letter I turn again to remind Your Paternity of what I have treated with Your Paternity at other times, both in person and also in writing — I mean, of my vocation to China or Japan, if it be to the greater glory of our Lord God. Your Paternity in Rome gave me no small good hope, promising me that doubtless in those parts I would do much service to our Lord God with my painting; but in the meantime you recommended that I commend it in prayer to the Lord; I say that I have done so and will do so also hereafter, and by God's grace I find myself much helped and strengthened therein. And to this end I have written to our Father Assistants of Italy and of Portugal, one each, that they may aid me with Your Paternity in this matter.",
   "Our Father, I pray you as much as I can, by the blood of Jesus shed for us, and by the merits of the Blessed Virgin Mary our Mother by grace, to be pleased to console me, if you know it to be the service of our common Lord — for this is what I hope, that is, the sanctification of His name as much as may be, according to the measure of the grace communicated to me by God.",
   "If for my sins there were any impediment to this, I know how to ask of you another grace, reserved for the answer to this one, very necessary to me if I am not mistaken; I make an end, with nothing else.",
   "I commend myself greatly to your Holy Sacrifices, and praying for you from the Lord a long and holy life full of merits."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant in the Lord — Francesco Ardoino. From Naples, March 5, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Francesco Ardoino",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, March 5, 1616",
  "date_only": "March 5, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 22,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2741"
 },
 "15": {
  "number": 15,
  "byline_raw": "Francesco Balione — Genoa, March 21, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 415",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The reason why until now I have not written to Your Reverence concerning the vocation to the Indies, it being more than five years that I have had this desire, has come partly from a certain vain respect, partly from not knowing the importance of writing, and also because, considering my weak strength and not looking to God who was urging me, I did not dare to write. Now at last, with the aid of the Lord and inspired by Him, I have resolved to make Your Reverence a sharer in the mercies of the Lord which He has used toward so vile and useless an instrument; and that you may more clearly understand my desire: the principal motive that moved me to embrace our Institute was the knowledge of the opportunity there was to go to the Indies. The end for which I am moved to go: first, the glory of God, having great desire that He be known; 2nd, to suffer something for His love, since for me He bore so many reproaches; finally, to render Him this mortal garb in witness to His Name, when He shall deign to make me partaker of so great a favor. I desire it greatly, nor have I words to explain. For the rest, I am pusillanimous, timid, and ignorant; nevertheless in the hands of Him who can do all things I am strong, and I fear nothing. I know not what else to say but: may He who knows and sees all things move our hearts. Meanwhile I resign myself into the hands of Your Reverence, awaiting, as from the hand of God's will, all that you shall judge in the Lord to be fitting for the greater glory of His Divine Majesty and my greater spiritual good."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Reverence — Servant and son Francesco Balione of the Company of Jesus. From Genoa, March 21, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Francesco Balione",
  "place_raw": "Genoa",
  "date_raw": "Genoa, March 21, 1616",
  "date_only": "March 21, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 21,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 24,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2742"
 },
 "16": {
  "number": 16,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, April 8, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 427",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Now is the time when Your Paternity can fulfill my long desire — either with this mission to Paraguay, if there be some impediment (as I understand) in any of those already chosen, or with those of Father Trigault. And not to hinder you, I do not believe Your Paternity will deny me at least one of them. I wish to console you with these missions, both for having written to you several times, and for having been among the first to ask Your Paternity to go to the Indies; if only (as I think) my faults do not prevent it. Yet Your Paternity could send me as a penance for them, which will be the greatest contentment I can ever desire or have in this life. With this I make you reverence, reminding you that: Parcat meis lachrymis (may he pardon my tears)."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. From the College of Naples, April 8, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 8, 1616",
  "date_only": "April 8, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 8,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 26,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2783"
 },
 "17": {
  "number": 17,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, April 15, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 433",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Now is the time when Your Paternity may remember me, as you have answered me so many times that you wished to do when the occasion arose — that is, by fulfilling my long and great desire for the Indies. My father, for love of God let Your Paternity console me in this alone; for the rest, ure et seca (burn and cut). For I neither wish nor desire any other consolation (as the Lord gives me to understand) in this life. In conclusion: Sapienti pauca (a word to the wise). I commend myself to you, praying you every happiness from Heaven."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. From the College of Naples, April 15, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 15, 1616",
  "date_only": "April 15, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 27,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2784"
 },
 "18": {
  "number": 18,
  "byline_raw": "Pietro Comentali — Naples, April 29, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 447",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "May the Lord Jesus second the holy desires of Your Paternity as He has seconded mine, with the greatest consolation of my soul (which I cannot express how extraordinary it has been). Let Your Paternity not doubt that I meanwhile shall not forget this so signal a favor and gift — namely, to have been made, beyond all my merit, one of the heralds of the greatness of God to the Paraguayans, by means of Your Paternity from the Lord — for I shall do nothing else, together with procuring the greater glory of God so as not to be ungrateful to Him, but pray always, in all the time of my life, and offer Him part of the labors of the voyages and of the conversions of those poor souls, that His Divine Majesty may console Your Paternity as He has consoled me by sending me to the earthly paradise of delights (for such I hold it), to Paraguay. Nothing else; I await your holy blessing. From the College of Naples, April 29, 1616. Concerning my relatives, Your Paternity shall have no trouble at all."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Pietro Comentali. Naples, April 29, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Pietro Comentali",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 29, 1616",
  "date_only": "April 29, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 28,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2785"
 },
 "19": {
  "number": 19,
  "byline_raw": "Ignazio Balami — Messina, June 2, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 462",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The ardor and the desire to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world, and where I may suffer most for love of Him who for me, an ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross, goes on growing so much that it does not permit me to linger long in consoling myself by unburdening it in some measure with Your Paternity. Know then that my desires, my thoughts, my consolations, and when I can, even my words, are in nothing else than this — holding myself, however, ever unworthy before the eyes of the Divine Majesty. Let Your Paternity forgive me if I trouble you, for I have no other refuge. I pray you, with what affection I know and can, to make me worthy of admission into the number of these saints who expose their lives for the Holy Faith; and let Your Paternity know that this befits me, that by this means I may begin to make satisfaction to that God whom I have so offended with the multitude of my sins and wickednesses. I have great hope of obtaining this, for I assure myself that Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, will know this need; and nothing else moves me but this, having great scruple at not having begun to ask since I promised it to the Lord when the General Confession was finished in the novitiate. I end without ever ending, anew in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ), praying you to console me and not to look upon my nothingness, for He who calls me will see to it that I may be a good subject for this ministry; and I shall never forget to pray the Lord that He grant you so much spirit and grace that you may be another Blessed Father Ignatius. To the Holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself with all affection."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant and son, who wishes you every good — Ignatio Balami. Messina, June 2, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Ignazio Balami",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, June 2, 1616",
  "date_only": "June 2, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 29,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2835"
 },
 "20": {
  "number": 20,
  "byline_raw": "Benedetto Amodei — Palermo, July 6, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 473",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The greeting that Your Paternity gave me through one letter to Father Cesare Cosso, then [Vice-Provincial], brought me as much contentment as certain hope of a thing so long desired is wont to bring, for such a greeting in reply to a resolved petition for the Indies is by us wont to be esteemed an acceptance among those who are destined for those most happy lands. I pray now that Your Paternity fulfill the greeting that you lovingly sent, and my desire, which every day grows ever more — and in this time particularly, with news of China and Japan. Permit me no longer to envy others, calling them most happy and fortunate, and myself most unhappy and unfortunate. May the Lord inspire Your Paternity to send me, as He has inspired me to desire to go.",
   "To satisfy in part with these few lines my great desire, I come now with as many more to fulfill the office I hold, since there is no other, of consultor in the Novitiate, giving Your Paternity a brief account of this house — which, by the grace of the Lord, seems to me to walk well in all the things it ought. The Novices universally are frequent in public and private mortifications and have need of the curb; in prayer they are so well [accustomed] that not only do they make it themselves and well, but they know how to teach it happily to others; they know how to speak and discourse well of spiritual things, and this they do not only in the Novitiate but keep up outside as well, in their studies, the Superiors remaining satisfied and content with them. All this seems to me to come from two things: from the good choice our Father Provincial has made of the best subjects, and from the good instruction of our Father Rector, to whom it seems to me there has been given the gift of teaching the […] with such assiduity and diligence that it amazes me; and what is more important, he gives them solid and substantial doctrines, and in particular and common discourses, [foreseeing], as is necessary, with his example; in short, he so conducts himself that he makes himself loved by all, and feared. This is what it has seemed good to me to write to Your Paternity, for the greater glory of His Divine Majesty, leaving unsaid the other Exercises, according to the custom of Novitiates, which have been made with great study, lest I be too long. To the Lord be that which may make me seek His glory in the Indies, and make happy the government of Your Paternity so well begun."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant — Benedetto Amodei. From Palermo, July 6, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Benedetto Amodei",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, July 6, 1616",
  "date_only": "July 6, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 6,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 31,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2744"
 },
 "21": {
  "number": 21,
  "byline_raw": "Alfieri, Giuseppe — Messina, July 7, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 473-1",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "While, if an ancient vine is not torn up from the root altogether (a thing not so easy to do as it seems), a master hand may well prune it, make it bleed again, mortify it, and cut it down so low that it no longer shows among the others, and it remains as if already stripped and buried — yet never, by that or any like pressure, kill it outright; rather, unless it spring again, rise up, and appear more vigorous than before. Your Paternity could cut from me on every side the tenderest shoots — as it seemed to me indeed when you called away those others, younger in age, newer and more tender in their vocation, who stood beside me as my chamber companions. You could make me weep, and at times languish, and mortify me so much that not even my name — poor though it be as refreshment for one oppressed — is heard among the others persevering for the Indies in your most loving letters, much less [directly or indirectly, or in whatever brief form] in answer to mine. I merit this, as is [truly] the case, for my demerits and sins. But that I should not rise again to make a new petition — that, thrust back, I should not return — I cannot keep myself from doing, my most beloved Father: [mercifully] there will live in me, thanks be to the Lord, the beginning and fountain which is He of all life, that desire for the glorious enterprise of the Indies, so deeply rooted these many years [in profound depths]. At least, not for my merit, or for any reason [as I say] [to feel] perhaps [resentful] as often as it might not seem so to Your Paternity to do it, but for your great fatherly charity I beseech you in the Lord, and in His Most Holy Mother Mary, not to leave me to languish in an unspeakable — not to say insuperable — pain, such as it is to be silent to one who cries out piteously; just as, at this point, I liken it to the pains of the damned, as you well know — David, when he said, Deus meus ne quando taceas a me, et assimilabor descendentibus in lacum (Lest Thou ever be silent to me, I shall become like them that go down into the pit). Thus at least I may understand from this whether writing does me good and gives hope, or whether I should cease, lest I show myself more importunate and insolent than fervent — committing all to the divine will, which must be carried out with the direction and the will of my Superiors. And with this I commend myself greatly to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your servant in Christ — Giuseppe Alfieri."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Alfieri, Giuseppe",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, July 7, 1616",
  "date_only": "July 7, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 33,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2743"
 },
 "22": {
  "number": 22,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Naples, December 9, 1616",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 506",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I believe it belongs to sons, in all their needs, to have recourse to their Father, so that according to their needs they may be provided for. If in temporal things it is so, with much reason — and more still — it is so in spiritual things, to the spiritual Father; and it seems to me that in this there occurs another thing as well, namely that not only our needs, but all other things too pertaining to the service and the greater glory of His Divine Majesty, must be made known to those Fathers — among which things are vocations. Having done this up to now, there occurs to me at present another thing, which belongs only to Our Father: the vocation to the Indies, a very great grace of God, and to be most greatly esteemed. It goes in this manner. Two years before I was admitted to the novitiate, or a little less, hearing talk of the things of the Indies — particularly of the martyrdoms — since from childhood I have always had the vocation to the Company, I felt certain interior movements, and was much taken with these things. Afterwards those movements kept growing within me, though I did not recognize the grace of God; these movements grew much with the occasion of certain missions which ours were conducting through Naples, with whom I used to go along to help at least to sing through the streets, to draw the people. When I felt those movements, since I did not recognize that grace, I said: if in small things I cannot master myself, how will I carry myself there? Entering the novitiate, and being in the Exercises, my desire grew greatly, and I began to know it was truly a thing of God. And so I offered myself to Him, and thanked Him. This caused me the consideration of the preciousness of a soul. One evening, if I am not mistaken, making the reflection of the prayer, my heart was kindled with this desire; and I did as usual, namely to say: if in small things I do not master myself, what will I do in greater ones? And having said this, I felt an answer within me: Potens est Deus de lapidibus (God is powerful, even from stones) — but interiorly. And this I have communicated to various superiors I have had, by whose counsel I have not written to Your Paternity before; but now, with the counsel of my [Father], I have written. For the [...] let us leave aside the other things concerning the same vocation, which I hope to write to you another time. All this I commend to God, saying, quid me vis facere (What wilt Thou have me do?), and to Your Paternity, His vicar. To Your Paternity I make a thousand reverences, and commend myself to you with all my heart."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Naples, the 9th of December [of the feast] of Blessed Xavier, 1616."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, December 9, 1616",
  "date_only": "December 9, 1616",
  "year": 1616,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 35,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2795"
 },
 "23": {
  "number": 23,
  "byline_raw": "Franceschi, Giacinto — 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 276",
  "salutation": [
   "Our Most Reverend Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Very Reverend Father, Our Father,",
   "The great longing which I have always had, and which I now feel within me more than ever, to go to the Indies has moved me to write these four lines to Your Paternity — especially now that the opportunity is favorable to go to Japan, toward which I aspire with all affection. I was ever desirous, even from a young age, of spending my life for the salvation of souls, and of shedding even my blood for Him who, in shedding His for them, was so lavish of it. So when I looked upon the image of the martyrs, I felt myself at once grow tender and burn with love for such a lot; and I said within myself: O, if God, who granted these so great a grace, would grant to me as well, I could do everything! And then there came to mind all the comforts, [pleasures], and paternal means, and I said, as I say anew now: away with kindred, honors, pomp — if only I too may at last be made worthy to suffer for my Jesus. This was the principal cause which inspired in me the vocation to the Company, and for this same reason God our Lord preserves me in it; for I never feel so consoled as with the consolation that fills me when an occasion offers to do something in aid of souls. And every time I have asked this (which I have often done, especially to Father Ottaviano Navarola while I was a novice, and to the Father Provincial while in the College), I have felt within me so great a consolation that, overflowing outwardly, I seemed to melt with spiritual joy; and though I did not obtain what I asked, it did not pain me — rather it helped me to grow more in spirit, so that, if it were God's will, I might obtain it one day. In prayer I never felt so much consolation as I felt then; then I found myself readier for obedience and for the exercises of the Holy Religion; and, to say all in one word, it always seemed to me I had made more profit in the spirit whenever I had disclosed such a desire to my Superior. I have always known, moreover, that this was communicated to me by the Lord God through the gifts which from that same God I have received, fitting me for such a vocation — namely, such health as I could not seem to desire greater; for I was never ill in the Religion except eight days of measles. Scarcity of food and fasting do me no harm, but rather good — fasting with the grace of the Lord through Lent; and finally from bodily and mental labors I have never felt harm to my health. I will end my writing, for it seems to me utterly impossible to show in writing the longing which Christ our Lord unworthily communicates to me — which might be to me a pain and a torment, seeming on the one hand not to satisfy the vocation fully on my part, though on the other hand it consoles me to have communicated it to Your Paternity, whose care I experience every day, how great it is toward me, most unworthy son — that care which seeks every means for my salvation. Into the hands of Your Paternity, therefore, I commit myself wholly, and at every nod of yours I am ready to obey; and if you wished to try this with mortification and with public and outward penances, I am ready to receive them."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most humble son in Christ — Giacinto Franceschi."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Franceschi, Giacinto",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "1617",
  "date_only": "1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": null,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 37,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2786"
 },
 "24": {
  "number": 24,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Sicily, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 226",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "For many pressing matters I have not written sooner to Your Paternity, as I ought — especially since I recalled with what kindness you greeted me, as though I were something. I know not what else to say to you, except that I thank you for the great charity you have shown toward me, a wretched man. And to inform you of my desire: I confess to Your Paternity that it keeps kindling ever more, and it seems to me to be the call of that Lord who infirma mundi eligit, ut confundat fortia (God has chosen the weak things of the world, to confound the strong) — and I desire nothing else but to correspond to so benign a Lord, who so lovingly calls me to so noble an enterprise. And although the instrument is altogether weak and powerless, still I am not confounded. Scio cui credidi. Potens est Deus (I know whom I have believed. God is able), etc. What else remains to me, then, but to pray you with all the affection of my heart, trusting in your great charity, for love of the Most Blessed Virgin Mary — in whose arms I have placed all my being, and this affair in particular — that you would put this my desire into execution, to the glory of God our Lord, and lift up this wretched man with your many Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your servant in the Lord — Marco Stella."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Sicily",
  "date_raw": "Sicily, 1617",
  "date_only": "1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": null,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 39,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2807"
 },
 "25": {
  "number": 25,
  "byline_raw": "Balami, Ignazio — Palermo, January 4, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 107",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Some months have already passed since my last was sent to Your Paternity — which I did so as not to trouble you, and all the more because I was certain that, should some occasion arise, Your Paternity, for your great charity and love toward your sons, would not have failed to console me, unworthy as I am — especially since it concerns a thing so important for my salvation. But the occasion of the captivity of my brother, Father Giuseppe Balami, has so enkindled my heart and inflamed it in such a manner that every day I weep, with a holy envy of him; for I would have wished it had happened to me, or at least would yet happen to me — the same — so that in this way a beginning might be made by me to satisfy, in some small part, my Lord for the multitude of my sins. Most loving Father General, they are such and so many that, if I do not come to shed my blood, I know not how I shall begin to make satisfaction; and Your Paternity must know that what I write to you is written with my face to the ground, and not without tears — though with great hope that the same Lord, hanging on the cross for my sins, may have inspired it in me, so that, while my age assists me, I may employ myself in His service, for I am already twenty-five years old. I pray Your Paternity in visceribus Iesu Christi (by the tender mercies of Jesus Christ) to remember me. I do not ask for the East Indies nor the West Indies, except where Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, shall judge that I may suffer much [and much]; for many, indeed very many, are my sins — and I assure Your Paternity that I shall never forget, though unworthily, amid labors and sweat, to pray the Lord to rain down upon Your Paternity all His graces. Do not abandon me, do not leave me, Your Paternity — for O, how it burns, how kindled is my soul! O, how it thinks of this, how gladly it speaks of this, and will never tire of speaking of it, since so God, who governs it, gives us to understand. I finish, most loving Father General, without ever finishing, standing with my face to the ground and deeming myself unworthy of so great a grace — not doubting, however, that the Lord who calls me will give me the grace and strength to serve Him. Humbly, then, I ask Your Paternity's blessing, praying for you from heaven every contentment, and commending myself greatly to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your humble and unworthy son — Ignazio Balami. From Palermo, the 4th of January 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Balami, Ignazio",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, January 4, 1617",
  "date_only": "January 4, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 4,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 40,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2830"
 },
 "26": {
  "number": 26,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Ferrara, February 13, 1617",
  "language": "Latin",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 118",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Most beloved Father: for the space of two years I have been held in a great desire to go toward the kingdom of China; and while the Reverend Father Giordano Cassino was provincial of the Venetian province, to me who begged it he gave authority to write to you about this matter. Behold, then, I humbly beseech you to grant me the permission to go thither, though unworthy. Nor refuse, for God has shown to me in prayer that in those parts I must die a martyr. quod Deus coniunxit homo non separet (What God hath joined together, let no man put asunder.) Do not say that I have not yet studied philosophy (for I am now giving myself to rhetoric), because He who teaches philosophy to all, teaches it also to me. Cum tradent vos, nolite cogitare quomodo, aut quid loquamini; dabitur enim vobis in illa hora quid loquamini. Non enim estis vos, qui loquimini, sed spiritus patris vestri qui loquitur in vobis. (When they shall deliver you up, take no thought how or what to speak; for it shall be given you in that hour what to speak. For it is not you that speak, but the Spirit of your Father that speaketh in you.) Nevertheless, do now what God wills. And let this be the first, and the last, letter on this matter — lest I trouble you. Farewell."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your servant in Christ — Antonio Rondonius of Forlì, twenty years of age. At Ferrara, on the Ides of February 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Ferrara",
  "date_raw": "Ferrara, February 13, 1617",
  "date_only": "February 13, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 42,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2773"
 },
 "27": {
  "number": 27,
  "byline_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo — Palermo, March 10, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 122",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Seeing the time approach when there is hope that missions to the Indies may be made, I am compelled by the desire to be one of those who shall be sent to those happy parts — I am compelled, I say, to refresh Your Paternity's memory, that I wait with the utmost longing for your call, hoping most firmly in the Lord, and in the charity which His Divine Majesty has placed in your heart toward us, His sons, that you will console me as soon as possible. I do not know what more to do to obtain this grace; I know nothing else to do but to pray His Divine Majesty to make me worthy of it — and since He has given me the desire, to bring it about that I fulfill what He wants of me. It is true He must do all, yet because He wills that we apply all diligence, I have recourse to you, Holy Father, as His minister, who represents His person to me — praying you to grant me what the Lord asks of me. I do nothing else but make an end, commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Vincenzo Fassari. From Palermo, the 10th of March 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, March 10, 1617",
  "date_only": "March 10, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 10,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 43,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2822"
 },
 "28": {
  "number": 28,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Naples, March 23, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 124",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Because blessed God does not fail, with His infinite mercy, as He has deigned to give me this holy desire to go to the Indies, so also to keep it in me, indeed to increase it ever more, giving me great hope of one day enjoying its fulfillment: so I believe it is not amiss to recall it to Your Paternity from time to time, accompanying it with the prayers of others and of mine; for by this means I hope to obtain this grace from His Divine Majesty, whom I have ever found so liberal toward me that I have good reason to promise myself as much, and more, for the future — although on my part I see no fitness at all, either for this or for anything else. And yet this very thing gives me great hope and confidence; for infima mundi eligit Deus, ut confundat fortia (God has chosen the meanest things of the world, to confound the strong). I pray Your Paternity, therefore, for the love of God and of the Blessed Virgin, to deign to remember me — though may the greater glory of God and of the Most Blessed Virgin always be done, to whom I commend this business and this letter. I make a thousand humble reverences to Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Naples, the 23rd of March 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, March 23, 1617",
  "date_only": "March 23, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 23,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 44,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2793"
 },
 "29": {
  "number": 29,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, April 21, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 136",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "At last I am no longer a child; my strength is whole, and my health excellent. I believe this is the time I have so long awaited, to go where God calls me — to the salvation of so many souls that are perishing; to gather that precious blood of Christ which is spilled upon the earth; to preach with my blood — O happy Diego, if that shall be! And I await it, my Father; I know well that, having ever been assured in this my vocation by so many most just ways through my superiors and spiritual fathers: I was born for the Indies — nor shall anyone take them from me; they are mine, and I must give a strict account of them before the tribunal of the great God. Thus has that infinite mercy and power been pleased: infimum mundi eligere (to choose the lowest thing of the world) for so great a ministry. Let Him do as He will. I know well that since Your Paternity is continually that which the Lord has [...], it will not fail me. Yet, not to fail myself — rather, to fail God — I know what is mine to do. And I [put myself] in the hands and at the feet of Your Paternity, with total indifference in all that you shall dispose of me. Then I remind you of what I proposed to you at other times: that I was born and entered this most happy and most mighty Society of Jesus to subdue to God not one city, or one kingdom, but a hundred thousand worlds, were there so many. I feel, Father, the blaze grown each day ever greater in my heart, and it inflames me to this noble conquest. Let Your Paternity now at last let me follow my Captain to the Indies, for it is time. He has opened to me an immense vastness; and O, what fair fruits await! The many difficulties which I know I shall have to bear do not frighten me, for I have one who will give me heart and strength. Rest assured, my Father, that there either the glorious death for Christ (O would it were so!) or something shall be. It suffices to make well known the God who has chosen me, a poor man, ne glorietur in conspectu eius omnis caro (that no flesh should glory in His sight). Most mercifully I only affirm to Your Paternity that it stands in your hand that I become an apostle — on God's part there is no lack; and I have already offered, and do offer, myself, most feeble instrument; I have no doubt at all — but in the hand of that Son qui vocat ea, quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt (who calleth those things that are not, as though they were), [...] what He wills. I finish. My Father, it is time; there is no need (I must study) to finish my studies here, for as many reasons as Your Paternity knows. And if indeed you wished me first to finish philosophy — which I wish were not so — at least, O my Father, [make] me sure. Open my heart, O most sweet and only Father, who are to me in the place of Jesus Christ. I burn, my Father, I burn, and I write it with tears in my eyes. Behold my heart in the hands and at the feet of Your Paternity, with all indifference; nor shall I depart from you ever, until I am fully, fully consoled. Do me this grace, my Father; for, though I am unworthy of it, I am your son. And I do not seek it for myself, but for Jesus Christ crucified and for the Holy Church of God. Grant it to me for love of the Most Blessed Virgin, for our Holy Father Ignatius, and for all the court of Heaven. I follow Your Paternity in the Holy Sacrifices and prayers daily; and I pray the Lord for every good for you, and the will to console me."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, the 21st of April 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 21, 1617",
  "date_only": "April 21, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 21,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 45,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2801"
 },
 "30": {
  "number": 30,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, April 21, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 139",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I ought not trouble Your Paternity further with new letters, since the last is still alive. But that thing which I put in it — namely that Your Paternity, if you would not send me now to the Indies, would at least assure me for after philosophy — was most foolish, and ungrateful to God. This is not what the fire I feel required, nor the confidence I must have. Had I need to be more assured in a matter so clear — which is [almost] a revelation, if not to me, at least to all my superiors and spiritual fathers? Enough. I repent. My Father, in the bowels of Jesus Christ: I see (let it be said, as it truly is, with all indifference) — I see, my Father, that now God wants me in the Indies, or in Japan, in so many occasions which Your Paternity knows well. I cannot wait for a better time. Now nothing is lacking to me; indeed, as it has pleased the infinite mercy of God [in everything I have]. My Father, I write — I know not how — with tears. Enough of what I have written to you. I present to you that most precious blood of Christ crucified; the salvation of infinite souls; and of my own soul. I shall be an apostle, for so God wills. The cause is not mine. I put all in the hands of Your Paternity, and myself at your feet. I await the most happy news. And meanwhile I do not cease to pray, as I have ever done, for Your Paternity. Help me, my Father, for you are my Father to me."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, the 21st of April 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 21, 1617",
  "date_only": "April 21, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 21,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 47,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2802"
 },
 "31": {
  "number": 31,
  "byline_raw": "Asiano, Vincenzo — Bari, April 29, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 143",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Since I entered the Society in particular because I understood that from there I could easily pass to the Indies to cooperate in some measure in the helping of those souls, and since that desire has grown day by day within me; nonetheless, because I understood that we coadjutor brothers are not so necessary in those parts, I did not wish to reveal myself to my Superiors. Now, since Your Paternity has deigned with a letter to encourage us to perfection, and especially to the help and winning of the souls of the Indies, I offer myself most promptly, if you so judge, to serve those good Fathers who will go to those parts; and meanwhile I shall pray blessed God that, as by His grace He has deigned to grant me this desire, so through no fault of mine He may not make me unworthy of it. And certainly, in order to make myself a more fitting instrument, and to acquire a greater store of spirit, I would gladly choose to stay in some novitiate. As for the rest, I commend myself to your holy prayers and sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, servant and son — Vincenzo Asiano. From Bari, April 29, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Asiano, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Bari",
  "date_raw": "Bari, April 29, 1617",
  "date_only": "April 29, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 48,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2736"
 },
 "32": {
  "number": 32,
  "byline_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo — Palermo, May 5, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 150",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Every greeting from these Fathers, given me on behalf of Your Paternity, kindles and inflames my heart the more to ask Your Paternity with greater insistence for the Indies I so desire; and at the same time I take no little consolation in seeing the remembrance Your Paternity keeps of consoling me, directing me as soon as possible toward those lands — for which I cannot but thank you with all my heart — and I ask of God Our Lord that He inspire you to grant me what He demands of me, as of the one who stands in His place and is the interpreter of His divine will. Then I turn to Your Paternity, setting before you my desire, and from you I await its immediate fulfillment. And I beg you most earnestly to condescend to what I have asked you, so that I may employ myself wholly and entirely, and in a certain particular way, for God Our Lord, suffering for Him and helping those souls for whom He chose to give His own life and shed all His blood. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy prayers and sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your unworthy son in Christ — Vincenzo Fassari. From Palermo, May 5, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 5, 1617",
  "date_only": "May 5, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 49,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2823"
 },
 "33": {
  "number": 33,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, May 12, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 158",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "At last I have received the most happy news for me, and so long awaited — may God be forever blessed. Now indeed all my desires are fulfilled, and, what matters most, by the one who is for me the interpreter of the divine will. O happy me! My Father! I have been left thunderstruck, and as if beside myself, by the infinite mercy of God — may He be blessed again and infinitely; I cannot contain myself. Forgive me, Your Paternity, for I cannot and do not know how to express what I feel. I know nothing else, except that I have placed myself in the hands of the good and great God, and I believe He has accepted me. He will know well how to make use of me. Let hell rage; I am in good hands, and I no longer fear contradictions. Only it remains (of which I have no doubt) that Your Paternity keep me continually at your feet; and with the holy Sacrifices and prayers obtain for me from the Lord, as the final completion, that heart and spirit which are necessary for such an enterprise. With which I make you most humble reverence, giving you infinite thanks."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, May 12, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, May 12, 1617",
  "date_only": "May 12, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 50,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2803"
 },
 "34": {
  "number": 34,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, June 16, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 186",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
